  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A strong coastal storm is expected to bring \npotentially significant impacts to the region around \nThanksgiving Day.\n\nAnother unseasonably mild day is forecast ahead of a strong \ncold front that will push offshore later today. Highs will be \nin the mid 60s to near 70 degrees. High pressure will briefly \nbuild in to the region in its wake, with much cooler conditions \nexpected Tuesday and Wednesday. Highs look to mostly be in the \n40s and 50s with lows Tuesday night in the 20s to low 30s.\n\nBeginning on Wednesday, the next potential storm of concern \nwill begin to take shape off the Southeast coast. A strong \nupper-low will shift eastward out of the central US, with strong\ndivergence aloft favoring surface cyclogenesis over either the \nSoutheast or off the coast. From there, the low is expected to \ntrack northward towards the Mid-Atlantic through Thursday. \nGuidance remains quite consistent on the development and rapid \ndeepening of this low, though some differences still remain with\nrespect to the track. With that said, guidance today has \nlargely shifted west and slightly slower with the northward \nprogression of the low. The EC-ENS and GEFS members have all \nreflected this shift, with the mean low center of both suites \neither inland or right along the coast. With this being said, \nthere remain a few members that are offshore, though the number \nof members is decreasing. While it is too soon to say for sure \nthat this trend will hold, the overall synoptic pattern does \nappear to favor this, with two H5 low centers featured on both \nthe deterministic EC and GFS, with one being over southern \nportions of the US and the other over portions of the Missouri \nValley before the energy largely consolidates over the eastern \ntier of the US. \n\nThe chance for wintry precipitation (even at onset) appears to \nbe fairly small even across the Poconos and adjacent portions of\nfar northern NJ. Precipitation onset now could be as late as \nThursday afternoon for these locations, and warm air advection \nwill have been underway for quite some time by that point. This \nappears increasingly likely to be an all rain event for all \nlocations within our coverage area.\n\nSpecific impacts are still somewhat uncertain and track \ndependent. However, it should be noted that confidence is higher\nthan usual for this range that significant impacts could occur \nareawide. Strong winds at the coast as well as inland, coastal \nflooding (potentially significant), and locally heavy rainfall \nare all possible. Strong winds (gusts 40+ mph inland and 50+ mph\nnear coastal areas possible) could lead to tree damage and \npower outages, even inland. Moderate coastal flooding is \npossible along the Atlantic coast and Delaware Bay. Given recent\ndryness and progressive nature of this system, freshwater \nflooding does not appear to be a significant concern, but this \nthreat will continue to be monitored as rainfall amounts come \ninto better focus. The greatest impacts right now appear \nprobable Thursday into Thursday night, with the low pulling away\nfrom the area on Friday.\n\nDetails will continue to come into better focus over the coming\ndays. Continue to monitor the forecast for the latest updates \ngiven the potential for significant impacts to the region from \nthis system over the Holiday. The Holiday weekend looks to\nfeature insignificant and seasonable weather.",
